Pipnode runtime dependencies
============================

Node icons are drawn with Pango and rely on fontconfig finding the
FontAwesome face -- glyphs in the U+F000..U+F8FF Private Use Area
have no fallback in standard system fonts and would otherwise render
as tofu.  On Debian/Ubuntu install:

    sudo apt install fonts-font-awesome

This ships /usr/share/fonts/truetype/font-awesome/fontawesome-webfont.ttf
under the family name "FontAwesome" (the v4 webfont), which is what
the worksheet's Pango font description matches against.

The text-to-speech (TTS) sink node shells out to two external tools:

  * piper  -- neural TTS engine; not packaged in standard Ubuntu
              repositories, install from upstream releases.
  * aplay  -- ALSA playback front-end; ships with the alsa-utils
              package on Debian/Ubuntu (`sudo apt install alsa-utils`).


Installing Piper
----------------

1. Grab the latest pre-built release for your architecture from
       https://github.com/rhasspy/piper/releases
   The Linux build is shipped as `piper_linux_<arch>.tar.gz` and
   bundles its own libonnxruntime, libespeak-ng and voice phonemiser
   data; nothing else needs to be on the system.

2. Extract the bundle somewhere stable -- the binary uses an
   $ORIGIN-relative RUNPATH, so the surrounding files must travel
   with it:

       mkdir -p ~/.local/share
       tar -xzf piper_linux_x86_64.tar.gz -C ~/.local/share
       # this creates ~/.local/share/piper/

3. Put the launcher on PATH:

       ln -s ~/.local/share/piper/piper ~/.local/bin/piper

   Make sure ~/.local/bin is in $PATH (it is by default on most
   Ubuntu installs).


Installing a voice
------------------

Piper voices are downloaded separately from the Hugging Face
collection at https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices.  The TTS
node defaults to the Lessac English voice; install it with:

    mkdir -p ~/.local/share/piper/voices
    cd ~/.local/share/piper/voices
    base=https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/resolve/main/en/en_US/lessac/medium
    curl -fsSL -O $base/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx
    curl -fsSL -O $base/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx.json

Both files (the .onnx model and its .onnx.json sidecar) must sit next
to each other.  Other voices follow the same layout under their own
language/speaker/quality directory in the upstream repo.


Adding more voices
------------------

Every .onnx file present in ~/.local/share/piper/voices/ shows up in
the TTS node's model dropdown automatically (labelled by its
"speaker-quality" suffix), so installing a new voice is just a matter
of dropping its two files in beside the existing ones.  The four
English voices Pipnode is most often demoed with are:

    base=https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/resolve/main/en/en_US
    cd ~/.local/share/piper/voices
    for path in amy/medium/en_US-amy-medium \
                lessac/medium/en_US-lessac-medium \
                libritts_r/medium/en_US-libritts_r-medium \
                ryan/medium/en_US-ryan-medium; do
        curl -fsSL -O "$base/$path.onnx"
        curl -fsSL -O "$base/$path.onnx.json"
    done

Voices for other languages live under sibling locale directories
(e.g. de/de_DE, fr/fr_FR, ...) using the same naming scheme.  Browse
https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices/tree/main for the full
catalogue.


Verifying
---------

A round-trip smoke test:

    echo "Pipnode is talking." \
        | piper -m ~/.local/share/piper/voices/en_US-lessac-medium.onnx -q -f - \
        | aplay -q -

If you hear the sentence, the TTS node is ready to use.
